{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Balaam lifted up, etc.</b> It was necessary to say ״וַיִּשָּׂא״, and it was also necessary to mention his name and not rely on what was mentioned in proximity. For according to what we have established earlier, Israel were surrounded by clouds and no eye could gain mastery over them. Therefore it says that Balaam lifted up, etc. — meaning, his prophecy was lifted up, for he was the prophet of the nations of the world and would see a prophetic vision. And therefore he was precise in saying ״בִּלְעָם״ to allude to the mastery of the Evil Eye, which was not in anyone but him. And by this he indicated that the verse speaks of a prophetic vision, and through this he attained the ability to see Israel, who were hidden in the clouds of glory." "<b>And he saw, etc., by its tribes, and it came upon him, etc.</b> Meaning that he saw in them two great matters: first — that each and every tribe dwelled separately; second — <b>and it came upon him</b>, etc., that the Shekhinah rested upon each tribe separately, its kingship at its head. And the reason that each tribe has its own level, either on account of the merit that one has more than another, or according to its number. He also alluded to the connection between these two matters according to the words of our Sages, of blessed memory (Bava Batra 99b), who said that by virtue of Israel being arranged in this manner, each tribe by itself, the Shekhinah dwelled upon them and they became a chariot for it. And this is what he was precise in saying: <b>and the spirit of Elohim came upon him</b>."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "Reading נְשִׂיאוּת עַיִן as 'mastery of the Evil Eye' (i.e., the power of the evil eye), consistent with rabbinic usage; the phrasing is slightly elliptical in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:39.045291582Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}